Because an exception can occur at any time within the try block before the OpenWrite() call, or the OpenWrite() call itself could fail, we aren't guaranteed that the file is open when we try to close it. The finally block adds a check to make sure that the System.IO.FileStream object isn't null before you call the System.IO.Stream.Close* method. Without the null check, the finally block could throw its own System.NullReferenceException, but throwing exceptions in finally blocks should be avoided if it's possible.

A database connection is another good candidate for being closed in a finally block. Because the number of connections allowed to a database server is sometimes limited, you should close database connections as quickly as possible. If an exception is thrown before you can close your connection, using the finally block is better than waiting for garbage collection.

