You also need to add a using directive to the top of the source file:

    using System.Threading.Tasks;

The using directives must be before any other statements in the file. Otherwise, it's a compiler error. You can run the program again and see the animation. That makes a better experience. Experiment with the length of the delay to match your taste.

The preceding code creates a set of spinning lines separated by a space. Adding the await keyword instructs the compiler to generate the program entry point as a method that has the async modifier, and returns a System.Threading.Tasks.Task?displayProperty=nameWithType. This program doesn't return a value, so the program entry point returns a Task. If your program returns an integer value, you would add a return statement to the end of your top-level statements. That return statement would specify the integer value to return. If your top-level statements include an await expression, the return type becomes [System.Threading.Tasks.Task1?displayProperty=nameWithType](xref:System.Threading.Tasks.Task1?displayProperty=nameWithType).

