 Fix is breaking or non-breaking   Non-breaking

 Enabled by default in .NET 10   No

 Applicable languages   C# and Visual Basic

Cause

An externally visible method dereferences one of its reference arguments without verifying whether that argument is null (Nothing in Visual Basic).

You can configure this rule to exclude certain types and parameters from analysis. You can also indicate null-check validation methods.

Rule description

All reference arguments that are passed to externally visible methods should be checked against null. If appropriate, throw an System.ArgumentNullException when the argument is null.

If a method can be called from an unknown assembly because it is declared public or protected, you should validate all parameters of the method. If the method is designed to be called only by known assemblies, mark the method internal and apply the System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleToAttribute attribute to the assembly that contains the method.

